[THOUGHT] Serum osmolality is 258 mOsm/kg — markedly low (normal: 275–295), confirming *hypoosmolar hyponatremia*. This, combined with serum Na 122 mEq/L, urine Na 45 mEq/L, and urine osmolality 334 mOsm/kg (inappropriately concentrated for hypoosmolar state), fulfills all four diagnostic criteria for SIADH:  
1. Hyponatremia (Na <135),  
2. Hypoosmolality (serum osm <275),  
3. Inappropriately elevated urine osmolality (>100),  
4. Elevated urine sodium (>20–30) in absence of diuretics or volume depletion.  
No evidence of adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism (TSH normal), renal failure, or volume depletion (though clinical exam incomplete, the lab pattern strongly favors euvolemic SIADH). All eight evidence domains are now complete:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — subacute confusion over days;  
2) Course — progressive, non-fluctuating;  
3) Associated symptoms — none focal or systemic;  
4) PMH — HTN only;  
5) Meds/allergies — unknown antihypertensive, no allergies;  
6) Social — 30-pack-year smoker, social alcohol;  
7) Targeted exam — disorientation to time/place, alert;  
8) Labs/imaging — CT normal, Na 122, serum osm 258, urine Na 45, urine osm 334, TSH/renal/glucose normal.  
No further information is needed to identify the primary pathophysiologic diagnosis.

[FINISH]

